  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nFriday - Saturday: Rainfall Details\n\nA broad corridor of weak mid-level isentropic ascent and \ntheta-e advection works into the region starting Friday \nafternoon, lifting north and waning through the afternoon on \nSaturday. This forcing should spawn a ribbon of scattered rain \nshowers that likewise lifts northward in this 24-hour window. \nRainfall amounts don't look to be exceedingly high (20-30% \nchance of seeing amounts over 1/4\"), but they could still be \nenough to put a damper on Halloween activities. Extended \nHRRR/REFS runs are not overly excited in the coverage of these \nshowers through Friday night--a bit of good news for those who \nhave early Halloween plans. Moving into Saturday, there is \nhigher confidence (60-70%) in there being some light shower \nactivity during the daytime hours, but the all important \nHalloween rainfall forecast remains more nebulous with the \ndecaying nature of the showers (PoPs in the 10-20% range). Best\nguess is that there will still be lingering showers come \nsunset, but most areas will stay on the drier side, especially \nwest of the Mississippi River.\n\n\nNext Week: Prolonged Stretch of Rainfall\n\nWhat is shaping up to be one of our more dynamic systems thus \nfar this fall remains on track to occur early next week, but \nlarge scale details are still coming into focus that will play a\nmajor role in the local forecast. While many solutions \nyesterday were focusing on the northern stream energy phasing \nwith the southern trough early in the week, the medium range \ndeterministic and ensemble solutions are coming in line with \ntheir AI counterparts and now favoring these two \nairstreams/troughs to remain out of phase, resulting in the \nformation of double barrel/disjointed low structure along the \nFront Range on Monday night. This pushes the corridor of \nheaviest rainfall to the west of the region and results in an \noverall faster progression of the system Wednesday onward. \n\nNo matter how you slice the forecast it still looks like a wet \nand cloudy few days starting Monday and going through Wednesday,\nit's mainly a question of whether we see a quarter to half an \ninch of rainfall over those few days (a near certainty in the \nensemble solutions) versus some of the higher outliers that push\n2-3 inches over the 3 days (10% chance). While seemingly \nunlikely at this stage, if the northern low trends stronger over\nthe coming days, a risk for stronger storms could creep into \nour area on Tuesday afternoon. Not even the AI guidance is \nshowing this possibility at this time, but seeing a warm sector \nof 60+ dewpoints wrapping around this cyclone in Minnesota \nshould get one's attention for early November!\n\n\nTemperatures Trends into Next Week:\n\nTemperatures fall back closer to seasonal norms (highs in the \nmid-50s, lows in the 30s) for today and Friday in the wake of \nlast night's cold front. However, warm air advection kicks in \nahead of next week's system by Sunday and temperatures should \nonce again trend 5-10 degrees above normal through Tuesday or \nWednesday before colder air arrives for the end of the week. \nGiven the influx of precipitation and uncertainties in the \nsynoptic structure of next week's system, there is naturally \nhigher than normal uncertainty in how temperatures will pan out \nfor Tuesday through Thursday. It does look more likely that lows\nwill be on the warmer side (forecast NBM lows Monday night are \nat or above our average highs for this time of year!) until the \ncold front arrives, though when that front comes through varies \nby 24-36 hours at this range.",
